 <h3>1.2&nbsp;&nbsp;Each re-send is counted</h3>
-<p>Coding CLIs resend the whole conversation with every request. TokenSaver’s rewriting is deterministic, so text shortened in one request is shortened again in every later request that carries it, and each of those requests adds the same removed bytes to <var>D</var>. <var>T</var><sub>est</sub> therefore counts input tokens not transmitted, summed over re-sends. It is not a count of distinct content removed. On two sample days in August the meter’s tally was 12.6 times (Claude Code) and 39.8 times (Codex CLI) the number of distinct characters removed. Those ratios mix bytes with characters and predate a 30 August change, so they illustrate the effect rather than measure a replay count.</p>
+<p>Coding CLIs can resend tool results as conversation history. With fixed input, command metadata, configuration and encoding, a carried result is rewritten consistently on qualifying requests. Compaction, server-managed history and changed settings can change which text is carried or rewritten. <var>T</var><sub>est</sub> counts byte/4 estimates of input tokens not transmitted, summed over qualifying requests. It is not a count of distinct content removed. On two sample days in August the meter’s tally was 12.6 times (Claude Code) and 39.8 times (Codex CLI) the number of distinct characters removed. Those ratios mix bytes with characters and predate a 30 August change, so they illustrate the effect rather than measure a replay count.</p>
 
 <h3>1.3&nbsp;&nbsp;The public counter</h3>
-<p>Each VibeRails installation with an API key reports its lifetime <var>T</var><sub>est</sub> to viberails.ai every 15 minutes. For each pair of API key and computer name the server keeps the largest total that pair has reported, and the public counter shows the sum of those values, refreshed at most every 15 minutes. It follows that:</p>
+<p>While an active VibeRails root backend is open, enabled publishing attempts to report its lifetime <var>T</var><sub>est</sub> using a configurable interval (15 minutes by default), when a usable API key and endpoint are available. For each pair of API key and computer name the server keeps the largest total that pair has reported, and the public endpoint sums those values with a 15-minute cache per server process. This describes inspected source, not an independent audit of deployed totals. It follows that:</p>
 <ul class="tight">
